                    ins_balanced:        `Balanced design across the five dimensions - defensible recommendation for the steering committee.`,
                    step01_title:                 'Master-Data Investment',
                    step01_text:                  'Helio Verde runs four legacy systems, each carrying its own version of customers, vendors, and materials. The COO asks: how much of the CO$ 8M ERP budget should fund master-data preparation, starting Month -3? Davenport (1998) and the textbook research are unanimous on this discipline.',
                    step01_low:                   '2% (CO$ 160k) — treat as IT plumbing line',
                    step01_low_fb:                'Under-investment in master data is the most predictable failure cause across two decades of practitioner literature. Garbage data in becomes garbage data in the new ERP, harder to fix.',
                    step01_med:                   '8% (CO$ 640k) — dedicated team, multi-month workstream',
                    step01_med_fb:                'Aligns with field benchmarks and the Magal-Word / Monk-Wagner guidance. Master-data discipline is the make-or-break determinant of ERP success.',
                    step01_high:                  '20% (CO$ 1.6M) — over-funded standalone programme',
                    step01_high_fb:               'Over-allocates the budget. Squeezes the change-management and integration lines that also need funding to succeed.',
                    step02_title:                 'ERP Doctrine',
                    step02_text:                  'The system-integrator engagement partner asks for the deployment doctrine. Helio Verde is mid-market, mostly standard CPG processes, with no software ambitions of its own. Pick a doctrine and defend it.',
                    step02_cloud_clean:           'Cloud + clean-core S/4HANA — extensions side-by-side',
                    step02_cloud_clean_fb:        'Defensible default for mid-market with mostly standard processes. Keeps the digital core upgradable; isolates extensions in side-by-side cloud services.',
                    step02_onprem_custom:         'On-premise + heavy customisation of the core',
                    step02_onprem_custom_fb:      'The Lidl 2018 cancellation (€500M write-off) is the cautionary case: seven years of customisation against unusual inventory-valuation methods the standard system handled differently.',
                    step02_best_of_breed:         'Best-of-breed apps integrated via APIs',
                    step02_best_of_breed_fb:      'Modern alternative, but for a mid-market firm with no integration team it imposes a permanent integration tax and concentrates failure at the API seams.',
                    step03_title:                 'Organisational Structure',
                    step03_text:                  'The IT architect must configure the org structure. Helio Verde Beverages is a single legal entity with two bottling plants (Costaria Norte, Costaria Sur) and one sales organisation. Configure now, or every report for the next decade is harder than it should be.',
                    step03_one_cc_two_plants:     'One company code, two plants, two distribution channels',
                    step03_one_cc_two_plants_fb:  'Mirrors the legal and operational reality. Modern trade and traditional trade as distribution channels enables clean profitability analysis by channel.',
                    step03_separate_ccs:          'Separate company codes per plant for autonomy',
                    step03_separate_ccs_fb:       'Misrepresents the legal entity. Forces inter-company postings for every cross-plant transfer and complicates statutory reporting.',
                    step03_flat:                  'Single flat structure, no plant or sales-org split',
                    step03_flat_fb:               'Loses the operational granularity needed to run the business. Profitability by plant or by channel becomes a heroic spreadsheet exercise.',
                    step04_title:                 'Sales Order — Credit-Limit Check',
                    step04_text:                  'Mercado Sur (modern-trade customer) confirms a 5,000-case Helio Cola 500ml order valued at CO$ 1.4M. Open AR is CO$ 4.2M; credit limit is CO$ 6M. The salesperson saves the sales order in SD. What happens?',
                    step04_pass_realtime:         'Real-time credit check — pass with CO$ 0.4M headroom',
                    step04_pass_realtime_fb:      'SD reads the live FI balance. The system enforces what the credit policy decided. The next order Mercado Sur places will see this exposure update in real time.',
                    step04_always_pass:           'Always pass the order to keep the customer happy',
                    step04_always_pass_fb:        'Bypasses the foundational AR control. Bad-debt accrues invisibly until quarter-end — exactly the legacy-system pain the COO is trying to leave behind.',
                    step04_nightly_batch:         'Run credit check nightly, like the legacy system',
                    step04_nightly_batch_fb:      'Replicates the legacy 48-hour visibility lag inside the new system. The integration promise was real-time enforcement, not faster batches.',
                    step05_title:                 'Available-to-Promise',
                    step05_text:                  'Inventory of Helio Cola 500ml at Plant 1000 is 4,200 cases against the 5,000 ordered. Production order PRD-2098 will deliver 1,500 cases in two days. Mercado Sur asked for delivery in 7 days. Configure ATP.',
                    step05_atp_proper:            'Confirm 5,000 cases in 6 days, netting incoming production',
                    step05_atp_proper_fb:         'ATP is the bridge from sales promise to operations reality. Reads on-hand stock, planned receipts, and committed allocations to give one credible date.',
                    step05_atp_shipnow:           'Confirm immediate delivery, ignore the production schedule',
                    step05_atp_shipnow_fb:        'Classic over-promise. The single most common reason customers receive a "system-said-yes; warehouse-said-no" disconnect is a weak ATP configuration.',
                    step05_atp_deny:              'Deny the order — inventory is short',
                    step05_atp_deny_fb:           'Ignores planned production. The point of integration is that PP and MM data feed SD in real time — your ATP must read it.',
                    step06_title:                 'Goods Issue — Automatic FI Postings',
                    step06_text:                  'The warehouse picks and dispatches 5,000 cases. A worker scans the delivery and posts Goods Issue in MM. Standard cost is CO$ 950k. What does the integrated system do automatically?',
                    step06_auto_postings:         'Auto-credit Inventory CO$ 950k, debit COGS CO$ 950k, post controlling document',
                    step06_auto_postings_fb:      'Three records, one event. No human posts the COGS journal. Integration turns the physical event into an accounting fact instantly.',
                    step06_human_journal:         'Wait for the AR clerk to post the journal next morning',
                    step06_human_journal_fb:      'Legacy thinking. The whole point of an ERP is that physical events trigger automatic FI postings. Manual journals re-introduce the four-system delay.',
                    step06_inventory_only:        'Reduce inventory only, post COGS at month-end batch',
                    step06_inventory_only_fb:     'Defers the COGS posting and breaks period-margin reporting. Profitability analysis cannot run truthfully until cost flow is real-time.',
                    step07_title:                 'Billing & Tax Configuration',
                    step07_text:                  'Billing fires from the delivery. Mercado Sur is a Costarian modern-trade customer; standard VAT applies. Output should debit AR CO$ 1.4M, credit Revenue CO$ 1.18M, credit Output VAT CO$ 0.22M. Pick the tax setup.',
                    step07_tax_proper:            'Standard Costarian VAT determined by SD pricing procedure',
                    step07_tax_proper_fb:         'Pricing procedure determines tax from customer master, material master, and ship-to country. EDI invoice goes out automatically to Mercado Sur.',
                    step07_tax_zero:              'Apply 0% VAT — treat all sales as exports',
                    step07_tax_zero_fb:           'Mis-configuration that creates a serious statutory issue at year-end. Tax-rule errors at billing are one of the documented failure modes the lab catalogue lists.',
                    step07_tax_manual:            'Have AR clerk type the VAT amount on every invoice',
                    step07_tax_manual_fb:         'Re-introduces the re-keying the integration was supposed to eliminate. Also breaks the audit trail by allowing humans to override system-determined tax.',
                    step08_title:                 'Cash Application & Deduction Management',
                    step08_text:                  'Twenty-eight days later, Mercado Sur pays via bank transfer. The remittance shows CO$ 1.35M against an invoice of CO$ 1.4M — a CO$ 50k freight allowance routinely deducted. The bank statement is uploaded into FI Cash Management.',
                    step08_park_discrepancy:      'Auto-match invoice; park CO$ 50k discrepancy in deduction-management workflow',
                    step08_park_discrepancy_fb:   'The system clears what it can, surfaces what it cannot. The AR clerk works the deduction queue rather than reconciling four systems.',
                    step08_reject_payment:        'Reject the payment — amount does not match',
                    step08_reject_payment_fb:     'Brittle. Real customers deduct freight, allowances, and disputed items routinely. Rejecting payment creates a customer-service incident the integration was supposed to prevent.',
                    step08_auto_close_loss:       'Auto-write-off the CO$ 50k as a sales-allowance',
                    step08_auto_close_loss_fb:    'Removes a control. SOX-grade controls require human review of deductions above a threshold; auto-write-off invites bad-debt leakage.',
                    step09_title:                 'Period Close & Profitability Analysis',
                    step09_text:                  'Month-end. The COO asks: profitability by SKU, by customer, by channel, by week. Today the legacy systems take a fortnight of spreadsheet work to answer. What does the integrated system do?',
                    step09_co_pa:                 'CO Profitability Analysis runs over SD, MM, FI postings; BW serves the dashboard',
                    step09_co_pa_fb:              'Same data, many module views. The "profitability by SKU by channel by week" question is answered by reading, not reconciling.',
                    step09_manual_excel:          'CFO assembles the answer in Excel from FI extracts',
                    step09_manual_excel_fb:       'Rebuilds the legacy pain inside the new system. Three full-time analysts go back to spending their week reconciling extracts.',
                    step09_fi_only:               'FI close only; analytics deferred to a future phase',
                    step09_fi_only_fb:            'Defers the analytics value the COO is paying for. CO and BW are not optional add-ons; they are how integration converts to insight.',
                    step10_title:                 'MRP-Driven Purchase Requisition',
                    step10_text:                  'Plant 1000 needs 10,000 kg of refined sugar for next week. Current stock is 4,000 kg; supplier lead time is 5 days; production starts in 8 days. MRP runs nightly. What does the system do tonight?',
                    step10_auto_requisition:      'Auto-create Purchase Requisition for 7,500 kg with preferred-supplier source',
                    step10_auto_requisition_fb:   'MRP is the bridge from production plan to purchase requisition. Net requirement, safety stock, and source list all read from master data.',
                    step10_email_buyer:           'Send an email to the buyer to investigate',
                    step10_email_buyer_fb:        'Re-injects manual processing into a workflow the system can run autonomously. The buyer is the wrong place to start a routine reorder.',
                    step10_no_action:             'Wait for the planner to notice the shortage manually',
                    step10_no_action_fb:          'Production runs out of sugar in eight days; the planner needs visibility today. Manual detection is exactly what the legacy system did.',
                    step11_title:                 'Release Strategy',
                    step11_text:                  'The CO$ 35k requisition needs approval. The configured release strategy is: ≤ CO$ 5k auto; CO$ 5k–100k plant manager; CO$ 100k–1M CFO; > CO$ 1M Board. Which workflow fires?',
                    step11_plant_manager:         'Plant manager approval workflow',
                    step11_plant_manager_fb:      'Matches the configured band. The release strategy is one of the architecture-native controls; it makes approval auditable and tamper-evident.',
                    step11_auto_release:          'Auto-release — keep buyers efficient',
                    step11_auto_release_fb:       'Skips a SOX-relevant control. Auto-release above CO$ 5k violates the configured threshold and creates a documented audit finding.',
                    step11_board:                 'Escalate to Board — be cautious',
                    step11_board_fb:              'Mis-applies the band. Over-escalation paralyses the workflow and causes plant managers to bypass the system entirely.',
                    step12_title:                 'Purchase Order — Commitment Posting',
                    step12_text:                  'The buyer converts the released requisition into a Purchase Order at CO$ 4.67/kg under the framework agreement. The PO is sent to the supplier via EDI. What happens in FI when the PO is created?',
                    step12_commitment:            'Commitment posting — budget encumbered against the cost centre',
                    step12_commitment_fb:         'No cash has moved and no invoice has arrived, but the budget is now reserved. Real-time budget visibility, not month-end Excel, is the integration value.',
                    step12_ap_now:                'Debit AP immediately for CO$ 35k',
                    step12_ap_now_fb:             'Wrong accounting. AP arises when the supplier becomes a creditor — at invoice receipt, not at PO. This breaks the trial balance.',
                    step12_nothing:               'No accounting impact until the goods arrive',
                    step12_nothing_fb:            'Loses the commitment-tracking that prevents over-commitment. Budget owners cannot see what is outstanding until receipts post.',
                    step13_title:                 'Goods Receipt & GR/IR',
                    step13_text:                  'Five days later the supplier delivers 7,500 kg of sugar. The warehouse worker scans the packing list against the PO and posts Goods Receipt in MM. Inventory is now an asset. What account is credited for the CO$ 35k?',
                    step13_gr_ir:                 'GR/IR clearing account — temporary holding until invoice arrives',
                    step13_gr_ir_fb:              'GR/IR exists because physical receipt and financial invoice arrive on different days. A persistently large GR/IR balance is a smoke signal for broken three-way matching.',
                    step13_ap_direct:             'Credit Accounts Payable directly',
                    step13_ap_direct_fb:          'Skips the matching step. AP must be created when the invoice is verified against the PO and GR — not when goods arrive.',
                    step13_cash:                  'Credit Cash — pay on delivery',
                    step13_cash_fb:               'Ignores payment terms (net 30). Pay-on-delivery destroys working-capital management and the negotiated supplier terms.',
                    step14_title:                 'Three-Way Match',
                    step14_text:                  'The supplier invoice arrives by EDI: 7,800 kg billed at CO$ 4.67/kg. The PO was 7,500 kg at CO$ 4.67/kg; the GR was 7,500 kg. Logistics Invoice Verification runs the three-way match.',
                    step14_block:                 'Block invoice for payment — quantity mismatch (7,800 ≠ 7,500)',
                    step14_block_fb:              'Exactly what the foundational AP control does. The block routes to AP for resolution; the supplier must issue a credit note or the over-billed quantity is rejected.',
                    step14_auto_pay:              'Auto-pay — supplier knows their inventory best',
                    step14_auto_pay_fb:           'Defeats the three-way match — the foundational AP control. Phantom liability would post and pay every time a supplier over-billed.',
                    step14_auto_correct:          'Auto-correct invoice quantity to GR quantity',
                    step14_auto_correct_fb:       'Hides the discrepancy from the supplier and from the audit trail. Mismatches must be visible and resolved, not silently overwritten.',
                    step15_title:                 'Segregation of Duties & Implementation Pattern',
                    step15_text:                  'Final design review. The proposed role for the procurement lead allows: create POs, post invoices, approve payments, maintain vendor master. SOX §404 and the COBIT 2019 framework set the boundary. The COO also asks for the implementation-pattern recommendation. Pick the package.',
                    step15_sod_split:             'Split duties + 24-month phased implementation, change-mgmt at 15%',
                    step15_sod_split_fb:          'Defensible synthesis. Buyer creates POs, AP clerk posts invoices, treasury approves payments, master-data team owns vendor master. Phased: finance + procurement first, then SD + MM + PP, then HR + analytics. Steering committee chaired by the COO, not the CIO.',
                    step15_sod_violation:         'Keep all four duties + 14-month big-bang to save time',
                    step15_sod_violation_fb:      'Direct SOX §404 violation plus the Hershey 1999 risk pattern. Big-bang concentrates risk into a single weekend; the role consolidation removes the foundational AP and AR controls that make the system trustworthy.',
                    step15_sod_partial:           'Manager-override on duties + best-of-breed via APIs to skip the ERP',
                    step15_sod_partial_fb:        'Override-based controls are weaker than split duties; auditors treat super-user paths as elevated risk. Best-of-breed via APIs imposes a permanent integration tax that mid-market firms cannot staff without an integration team.',
                    chart_current:                'Current'
                },
